Queue<Anon> -- CS 452 HW1
=========================

Files
-----
  deq.h        the given interface; unchanged
  deq.c        the implementation
  error.h      the given WARN/ERROR macros; unchanged
  main.c       the test suite
  test.out     its output, from "make run"
  valgrind.out valgrind's report, from "make valgrind"
  GNUmakefile  this directory's makefile; ../GNUmakefile has the rules

Building
--------
This directory's makefile needs ../GNUmakefile, the course makefile.
With it:

  make           builds ./deq, from deq.o and main.o
  make run       builds and runs the test suite
  make valgrind  runs the test suite under valgrind
  make clean     removes the built files

./deq exits 0 iff every test passes.

Design
------
This queue is a pair of end pointers, ht[Head] and ht[Tail], plus a
length.  Each node holds a pair of neighbor pointers indexed the same
way: np[Head] points toward the head, np[Tail] toward the tail, and a
missing neighbor is 0.

Indexing both arrays by the same End is what makes the ends symmetric.
put, ith, get and rem are each written once, in terms of an end e and
its opposite other(e); deq_head_xxx() calls the one implementation with
Head and deq_tail_xxx() calls it with Tail.  unput(), which splices a
node out for both get and rem, is itself a loop over the two ends, so
even unlinking is coded once.

The queue owns its nodes and nothing else.  It never copies, inspects,
or frees the data it is handed; deq_del()'s map function is how a
caller disposes of data it owns.

Semantics
---------
  put   appends onto an end; len++
  get   removes from an end and returns its data, or 0 if empty; len--
  ith   returns the data i nodes in from an end, 0-based; len unchanged
  rem   removes the node nearest an end whose data == d (pointers are
        compared, not contents) and returns d, or 0 if not found;
        len-- iff found
  map   applies f to every datum, head to tail
  del   maps f (if given) over the data, then frees the queue
  str   returns a freshly allocated, space-separated rendering; with a
        function, it is applied to each datum and its result freed;
        without one, the data are assumed to be strings

Errors
------
A zero queue or a failed malloc() cannot be recovered from: ERROR()
reports it and exits.  An out-of-range index is a recoverable mistake:
WARN() reports it, naming the index and the legal range, and 0 is
returned.  Data that is simply absent is a legitimate result and returns 0.

Testing
-------
main.c runs 54 tests: the empty queue and its edge cases, puts and gets
at each end and interleaved, indexing from both ends including
out-of-range and negative indices, rem from the middle, from each end,
of an absent datum and of one held twice (showing head_rem and tail_rem
pick opposite occurrences), map and str with and without a function,
and del over heap data.  Six warnings on stderr are expected: they are
the out-of-range tests demonstrating the error messages.

Memory
------
Every node is freed by get, rem, or del, and every string deq_str()
builds is freed by its caller. "make valgrind" reports no leaks and no
errors; valgrind.out is that report.

AI Use
------
Claude Code was used to test and format code that I wrote, as well as
to correct and expand comments where needed. It was also used for tutor
functionality to refresh concepts (pointers) that I was rusty on.
